post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'

VelaTerm is installed as a pacman-managed package.

  Wayland: this package installs the binary without the AppImage's bundled Debian
  libraries, so it runs as a native Wayland client instead of under XWayland. Do not
  set GDK_BACKEND to work around anything -- it is inherited by every shell and agent
  VelaTerm spawns.

  CLI shims: on first launch VelaTerm writes 'vela', 'vspawn' and 'vopen' helper
  commands into a directory on your PATH.

  Auto-update: the built-in Tauri updater fetches from velaterm.com and would try to
  replace /usr/bin/velaterm, which it cannot write. Turn auto-update off in Settings so
  it stops erroring, and update through this package instead.

  Remote access: off by default. When enabled it exposes a shell on your network, so
  keep it bound to loopback and reach it over your tailnet:

    velaterm --serve --port 8799 --local-http
    tailscale serve --bg 8799

  That gives a real Let's Encrypt certificate on your *.ts.net name instead of the
  self-signed one, and leaves the tailnet as the only path in.

  Headless hosts: the same binary serves without a display server, but this package
  pulls in webkit2gtk for the desktop window. On a server, build upstream with
  --no-default-features for a GUI-less vela-server instead.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}
